Topic: What is your role in the group and has it changed over the semester? If there were any challenges, how did you deal with them? 

By Week 9, our group had completed the mock presentation and received feedback. The presentation went smoothly as each member completed their respective parts. However, an issue raised by my lecturer was that some boxplots were affected by extreme outliers, which compressed the central distribution and made group comparisons less clear. Although this section was initially handled by another member, the group worked together to improve it, and I actively helped refine the outlier handling.
I felt encouraged that the mock presentation was completed without major issues, and I appreciated the lecturer’s feedback as it was relevant for improvement. It reminded me that a graph can be technically correct but still be ineffective if it does not communicate the findings clearly.
As with all projects, our progress had strengths and weaknesses. I evaluated that one strength was our willingness to support one another instead of leaving the issue to the member who first handled that section. However, our report progress was slow due to other concurrent assignments, showing that time management remained a challenge.
I analysed that my role had shifted from focusing mainly on my own EDA section to contributing more flexibly to the group’s overall work. The outlier issue showed me that teamwork also involves recognising when a section affects the clarity of the whole project. It also taught me that visualisation decisions should be guided by communication, not just technical correctness, because the purpose of EDA is not only to display results but to make patterns understandable to the audience.
In conclusion, I learned that my role can change depending on the group’s needs. Moving forward, I will practise presenting with less reliance on my script, while the group will prioritise completing the report by Week 10 and reviewing our visuals for relevance and clarity.
